{# The totals box beside a sell-side document's line table: subtotal, shipping, charges, tax,
   total. The order's and the quote's, which were the same sixty lines twice.

   NOT the invoice's. An invoice carries its money as a <tfoot> of the line table itself — with
   rows a document appends after the total — the invoice's Amount Paid and Balance Due — because an
   order is never paid, its invoices are — so it is the invoice, and a
   different shape. Three implementations of one arithmetic is still the finding; this closes two
   of them. The third closes when the arithmetic moves to a DocumentTotals view-model built in the
   controller, which is what the row shape below is already written to accept:

       {subtotal, shippingRows[], beforeTaxRows[], taxRows[], totalTax, afterTaxRows[], total}

   with every amount nullable and null meaning TBD. Two things in this file are the reason that
   view-model is not simply this file's parameter list yet, and both are real: the invoice's
   charge labels carry a `× quantity` span INSIDE the label, and its Total Tax row shows
   unconditionally — BOTH are now this box's behaviour, so the invoice uses it too. Whoever builds
   the view-model has to decide both, and neither decision belongs in a template.

   The OUTER wrapper div stays with each caller. The two disagree about its inline style by a
   trailing semicolon, and a parameter existing only to carry one character of CSS punctuation is
   drift with extra steps.

   Parameters
   ----------
   subtotal       required  the document's subtotal ALREADY carrying its main-line charges, or
                            null when unstated. Subtotal is the sum of what the table above lists,
                            or the figures below stop adding up to the Total.
   shippingLines  required  one row per shipping line, labelled exactly as it was entered — a
                            document can carry a carrier charge, a fuel surcharge and a
                            residential fee, and each is its own row. Pass [] for none, and a
                            stand-in row is printed rather than nothing: a shipping-free order
                            still has to say so.
   beforeTaxRows  required  charge rows placed above the tax rows, each {label, amount}. Pass [].
   afterTaxRows   required  charge rows placed below Total Tax, each {label, amount}. Pass [].
                            Ad-hoc tax an admin typed is a TAX line and is already listed with the
                            calculated ones, not hanging below Total Tax unexplained.
   taxLines       required  the calculated tax rows, each {label, rate, amount} with a nullable
                            rate and an optional slug. Pass [] for none.
   tax            required  the document's total tax, or null when unstated.
   total          required  the document's total, or null when unstated.
   allowUnstated  optional  true on a quote. Wherever an amount is null it reads TBD rather than
                            $0.00, and the shipping stand-in reads TBD too: shipping is the one
                            figure a quote may leave unstated, and a stated $0.00 would tell the
                            customer it ships free (#254/#255). An order is priced by definition,
                            so its stand-in is a real $0.00. Defaults to false.
   taxRowIds      optional  true to key each tax row `tax-line-<the calculator's own slug>`, so a
                            specific row can be pointed at. The order carries them; the quote
                            never has. Defaults to false.

   `shipping-fee-row` is the hook the quote PDF has carried since #254 and the one the order
   screen, the quote screen, the invoice screen and the invoice PDF all carry now, so the row is
   addressable by the same name wherever a sell-side document renders it. A semantic hook with no
   styling, like `fee-line-row` in the table above. #}

{% set unstated = allowUnstated|default(false) %}
{% set rowStyle = 'display:flex; justify-content:space-between; padding:8px 0; font-size:0.88rem; border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-light);' %}

<div style="width:380px; background:#fff; border:1px solid var(--line-light); border-top:0; border-radius:0 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md); padding:20px 24px; box-shadow:var(--shadow-sm);">
    <div style="{{ rowStyle }}">
        <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:600;">Subtotal:</span>
        <strong style="color:#334155;">{{ subtotal is not null ? '$' ~ subtotal|amount : 'TBD' }}</strong>
    </div>
    {% if shippingLines is empty %}
        <div class="shipping-fee-row" style="{{ rowStyle }}">
            <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:600;">Shipping Fee:</span>
            <strong style="color:#334155;">{{ unstated ? 'TBD' : '$0.00' }}</strong>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
    {% for shippingLine in shippingLines %}
        <div class="shipping-fee-row" style="{{ rowStyle }}">
            <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:600;">{{ shippingLine.label }}:</span>
            <strong style="color:#334155;">${{ shippingLine.amount|amount }}</strong>
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
    {% for feeLine in beforeTaxRows %}
        <div style="{{ rowStyle }}">
            <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:600;">{{ feeLine.label }}{% if feeLine.quantity|default(1) != 1 %} <span style="font-weight:400; color:#64748b;">&times; {{ feeLine.quantity|qty }}</span>{% endif %}:</span>
            <strong style="color:#334155;">${{ feeLine.amount|amount }}</strong>
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
    {% for taxLine in taxLines %}
        <div{% if taxRowIds|default(false) %} id="tax-line-{{ taxLine.slug|default('unslugged') }}"{% endif %} style="{{ rowStyle }}">
            <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:600;">{{ taxLine.label }}{% if taxLine.rate is not null %} {{ (taxLine.rate * 100)|number_format(0) }}%{% endif %}:</span>
            <strong style="color:#334155;">${{ taxLine.amount|amount }}</strong>
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
    {# Always shown, including a genuine $0.00. It used to hide at zero here while the invoice
       showed it unconditionally, which is the difference that kept the invoice on its own totals
       template. Ruled 2026-09-12: show it, and the three converge. A null tax is TBD rather than a
       hidden row, same as Subtotal and Total Amount (#254). #}
    <div style="{{ rowStyle }}">
        <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:800;">Total Tax:</span>
        <strong style="color:#334155;">{{ tax is not null ? '$' ~ tax|amount : 'TBD' }}</strong>
    </div>
    {% for feeLine in afterTaxRows %}
        <div style="{{ rowStyle }}">
            <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:600;">{{ feeLine.label }}{% if feeLine.quantity|default(1) != 1 %} <span style="font-weight:400; color:#64748b;">&times; {{ feeLine.quantity|qty }}</span>{% endif %}:</span>
            <strong style="color:#334155;">${{ feeLine.amount|amount }}</strong>
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
    <div style="display:flex; justify-content:space-between; padding:14px 0 4px; font-size:1.05rem;">
        <span style="font-weight:800; color:var(--ink);">Total Amount:</span>
        <strong style="font-weight:800; color:var(--admin-blue-hover);">{{ total is not null ? '$' ~ total|amount : 'TBD' }}</strong>
    </div>
    {# Rows a document adds after the total. Generic on purpose: this box does not know that an
       invoice is paid and an order is not — it is handed rows and prints them. The invoice passes
       Amount Paid and Balance Due; the other two pass nothing. #}
    {% for row in afterTotalRows|default([]) %}
        <div style="{{ rowStyle }}">
            <span style="color:#64748b; font-weight:{{ row.strong|default(false) ? 800 : 600 }};">{{ row.label }}:</span>
            <strong style="color:#334155;">${{ row.amount|amount }}</strong>
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
</div>
